-		<English>[TAB]When discussing "Daoism," one must be careful to distinguish between the philosophy of Dao and the religion of Dao. The philosophy of Dao came first. According to tradition, the philosopher Lao-Tsu lived in the 4th Century BC. He wrote the "Dao Te Ching," which can be roughly translated as "The Book of the Way and its Virtue." [PARAGRAPH:2]The Dao Te Ching is written in verse. It is extremely subtle and difficult to understand. Many scholars have spent years trying to interpret it. The book can be (very primitively and probably inaccurately) summarized as follows: the "Dao" is what exists before everything else. It is formless, and from it all things are formed. Man must aspire to simplicity; only by discarding the useless trappings of culture and civilization can man return to his original state of emptiness - Dao - when all is possible. According to Lao-Tsu, "the sage has no heart of his own" - no intentions, no preconceptions, and no agenda. [PARAGRAPH:2]The religion of Daoism is based upon worship of Lao-Tsu as a deity. According to Daoist religious tradition, in 142 AD Lao-Tsu appeared to Zhang Daoling, a hermit, and told him that the world was coming to an end, and those who followed Lao-Tsu would go on to another, better life. Further, Lao-Tsu's followers would have access to the celestial powers that control the fate of mankind. Zhang preached Lao-Tsu's words and gained many converts.[PARAGRAPH:2]Over time, Daoism split into a number of schools. Some believed that they could change the world through talismans, alchemy, and magical spells. Some sought to achieve immortality through a Buddhist-like enlightenment. A variety of gods from Chinese folklore and Buddhist tradition were incorporated into the religion.[PARAGRAPH:2]Daoism spread widely throughout China, and it was the official state religion for a thousand years, until the end of the Qing Dynasty in 1911. The Communists virtually eradicated the religion in China during the Cultural Revolution (1960-76), when many of the temples were destroyed and the monks and priests sent to "reeducation" camps. In the 1980s China restored some religious tolerance, and Daoist temples are slowly being rebuilt and repaired. [PARAGRAPH:2]It is believed that many Chinese still practice Daoism in some form, despite the government's restrictions. Daoism is also practiced by some 31 million people outside of China, the largest group being in Taiwan. Its influence can be felt world-wide in the practices of acupuncture, herbalism, feng shui, and martial arts. </English>
+		<English>[TAB]Daoism or Taoism is a diverse philosophical and religious tradition indigenous to China, emphasizing harmony with the Dao _ (pinyin: d�o; Wade-Giles: tao). With a range of meaning in Chinese philosophy, translations of Dao include 'way', 'road', 'path', or 'technique', generally understood in the Daoist sense as an enigmatic process of transformation ultimately underlying reality. Daoist thought has informed the development of various practices within the Daoist tradition and beyond, including forms of meditation, astrology, qigong, feng shui, and internal alchemy. A common goal of Daoist practice is self-cultivation, a deeper appreciation of the Dao, and more harmonious existence. Daoist ethics vary, but generally emphasize such virtues as effortless action, naturalness, simplicity, and the three treasures of compassion, frugality, and humility.[PARAGRAPH:1]The core of Daoist thought crystallized during the early Warring States period (c. 450 - c. 300 BCE), during which the epigrammatic Dao de jing and the anecdotal Zhuangzi-widely regarded as the fundamental texts of Daoist philosophy-were largely composed. They form the core of a body of Daoist writings accrued over the following centuries, which was assembled by monks into the Daozang canon starting in the 5th century CE. Early Daoism drew upon diverse influences, including the Shang and Zhou state religions, Naturalism, Mohism, Confucianism, various Legalist theories, as well as the I Ching and Spring and Autumn Annals.[PARAGRAPH:1]Although Daoism and Confucianism developed significant differences, they are not seen as mutually incompatible or exclusive. The relationship between Daoism and Buddhism upon the latter's introduction to China is characterized as one of mutual influence, with long-running discourses shared between Daoists and Buddhists; the distinct Mahayana tradition of Zen that emerged during the Tang dynasty (607-917) incorporates many ideas from Daoism.[PARAGRAPH:1]Many Daoist denominations recognize deities, often ones shared with other traditions, which are venerated as superhuman figures exemplifying Daoist virtues. They can be roughly divided into two categories of "gods" and xian (or "immortals"). Xian were immortal beings with vast supernatural powers, also describing a principled, moral person. Since Daoist thought is syncretic and deeply rooted in Chinese culture for millennia, it is often unclear which denominations should be considered "Daoist".[PARAGRAPH:1]The status of daoshi, or 'Daoist master', is traditionally attributed only to clergy in Daoist organizations, who distinguish between their traditions and others in Chinese folk religion. Though generally lacking motivation for strong hierarchies, Daoist philosophy has often served as a theoretical foundation for politics, warfare, and Daoist organizations. Daoist secret societies precipitated the Yellow Turban Rebellion during the late Han dynasty, attempting to create what has been characterized as a Daoist theocracy.[PARAGRAPH:1]Today, Daoism is one of five religious doctrines officially recognized by the Chinese government, also having official status in Hong Kong and Macau. It is considered a major religion in Taiwan, and also has significant populations of adherents throughout the Sinosphere and Southeast Asia. In the West, Daoism has taken on various forms, both those hewing to historical practice, as well as highly synthesized practices variously characterized as new religious movements.[PARAGRAPH:2]"Dao" and "Tao" are different romanized spellings of the same Chinese word: _.[NEWLINE]-"Dao" is the romanized spelling in the Hanyu Pinyin system, officially adopted in China in the 1950s, which has largely replaced Wade-Giles romanization.[NEWLINE]-"Tao" is the romanized spelling in the Wade-Giles system, which was predominant in English-speaking countries until the late 20th century, and remains in use for certain terms with strongly established spellings.[PARAGRAPH:1]The Standard Chinese pronunciation of _ is /tau_/.[NEWLINE]-In Standard Chinese, the initial consonant, /t/, is not voiced like the /d/ in the English word "do".[NEWLINE]-In Standard Chinese, the initial consonant, /t/, is not aspirated like the /t_/ in the English word "to".[PARAGRAPH:1]Neither an English pronunciation like /da_/ (an English pronunciation of "Dao") nor an English pronunciation like /t_a_/ (an English pronunciation of "Tao") is the same as the Standard Chinese pronunciation of _.[PARAGRAPH:1]These differences in the initial consonants used in English and Standard Chinese may contribute to there being different methods of romanizing Chinese, which consequently may confuse English speakers when encountering both "Dao" and "Tao" spellings for the same Chinese term.[PARAGRAPH:1]One authority calls the pronunciation with a _t_ as in "tie" (with a /t_/) to be a "mispronunciation" originally caused by the "clumsy Wade-Giles system", which misled most readers. Standard Chinese phonology does not have the same sound inventory as English phonology; the Wade-Giles romanization system provides spellings in the Latin alphabet, but they are not meant to indicate an exact English pronunciation in the same way as though they were English words.</English>
 	</TEXT>
